<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vindolanda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chesterholm</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vindolanda was a Roman auxiliary fort in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, just south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>. Built around 85 CE, before the wall, it was garrisoned by auxiliary units of Tungrians and Batavians. Excavations directed by Robin Birley from 1973 recovered the Vindolanda tablets, thin wooden writing tablets of around 100 CE that preserve letters, lists and accounts and give a rare picture of daily life on the frontier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vindolanda.jpg" alt="Vindolanda" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike Bishop · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kastell_und_Vicus_von_Vindolanda,_Luftbild,_2010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The fort lies on a hill near the village of Bardon Mill, where it guarded the Stanegate, the Roman road from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solway-firth">Solway Firth</a>. The name is probably Celtic, meaning white lawn or similar, and an altar records a group called the Textoverdi, who may have been the native people of the area. Archaeologists divide the site into nine periods: five successive forts of wood and turf, buried up to 4 metres deep in waterlogged, oxygen-free soil that has preserved large amounts of organic material, and later stone forts above them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first unit, the Cohors I Tungrorum from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Gallia Belgica</a>, arrived after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Gnaeus Julius Agricola</a>'s conquest of the north and built the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> fort, dated by Samian ware and a coin of Vespasian. One tablet, a troop roster, records 746 men and 6 centurions, of whom 295 men and one centurion were then at Vindolanda, 15 of them sick and 10 out of action with conjunctivitis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohort</a> returned in or after 105, and a large timber building may have housed Hadrian while he oversaw the first stages of his wall in 122. Around 128 the Tungrians moved to the new fort of Vercovicium, Housesteads.</p><p>In the 210s a new stone fort was built for the Cohors IV Gallorum equitata, with a civilian settlement, or vicus, to the west, and most of the remains visible today belong to this period or later. Fort and village were largely abandoned about 285 for reasons unknown, the fort was rebuilt about 300 without its village, and it was roughly repaired about 370. There is no evidence that Roman occupation ended suddenly in 410.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Eric Birley bought the house at Chesterholm in the 1930s, and his sons Robin and Anthony and later his grandson Andrew Birley continued the summer excavations. Besides some 850 ink tablets, finds include over 160 boxwood combs, most of a military tent and the largest known group of Roman shoes. The Vindolanda Trust, a charity founded in 1970, runs the site and its museum, which has reconstructed buildings in its gardens.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vindolanda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Vindolanda</b> tablets, letters on wood from a fort near Hadrian's Wall, reveal the routine of this administration around 100.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Tungrian cohorts served across the empire, most famously on Hadrian's Wall and at <b>Vindolanda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tungri">Tungri</a></li></ul></section>
